@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SQL Queries";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Queries
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL Queries are used to retrieve, filter, sort and
            manipulate data stored in SQL Server databases.
        </p>

        <p>
            In this module, we will learn commonly used SQL queries
            and clauses that help us work efficiently with database
            data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS SQL QUERY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a SQL Query?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A SQL query is a statement written using Structured Query
            Language (SQL) to communicate with a database.
        </p>

        <p>
            Using SQL queries, we can retrieve data, filter records,
            sort results, group data and perform calculations.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC QUERY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic SQL Query
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query retrieves all columns and all records from the
            Students table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- COMMON QUERY CLAUSES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common SQL Query Clauses
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Clause</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SELECT</strong></td>
                        <td>Retrieves data from a table.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FROM</strong></td>
                        <td>Specifies the table from which data is retrieved.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>WHERE</strong></td>
                        <td>Filters records based on a condition.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>ORDER BY</strong></td>
                        <td>Sorts the query result.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>GROUP BY</strong></td>
                        <td>Groups rows having similar values.</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>HAVING</strong></td>
                        <td>Filters grouped results.</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- WHERE EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example – WHERE Clause
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age &gt; 21;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query returns only those students whose age is
            greater than 21.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- ORDER BY EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example – ORDER BY
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
ORDER BY StudentName ASC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query sorts the student records by StudentName in
            ascending order.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- QUERY PROCESS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            How a SQL Query Works
        </h2>

        <ol>

            <li class="mb-2">
                The query is written using SQL syntax.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                SQL Server parses and validates the query.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                SQL Server determines how to execute the query.
            </li>

            <li>
                The requested result is returned to the user.
            </li>

        </ol>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SQL queries are used to communicate with databases.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SELECT is commonly used to retrieve data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    WHERE is used to filter records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    ORDER BY is used to sort results.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    GROUP BY is used to group records.
                </li>

                <li>
                    HAVING is used to filter grouped results.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we introduced SQL Queries and learned
                the purpose of commonly used clauses such as SELECT,
                FROM, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY and HAVING.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


